#!/usr/bin/env bash
#MISE description="Hook body: remind once when the instruction surface changed under a running session (reads the hook payload on stdin; pointer-only)"
#
# CLOUD-187. A session loads its hook wiring and reads its instruction files ONCE,
# at start. When the contract then changes on `main` — a new guard lands, a
# workflow rule is rewritten — the running session keeps executing the old
# contract with no signal that it drifted. Measured: `issue-guard` landed
# mid-session and a live session readied a PR whose branch and commits carried no
# `CLOUD-<n>` key, because that hook was never in that session's wiring; the same
# session rebased across several instruction-file changes and re-read none.
#
# The landing-path gates are unaffected — they run from the TREE, not the session.
# What binds only through the session-start snapshot is CONDUCT: how to sequence
# the lifecycle, board discipline, what a commit must carry. So this is
# feedforward with a trigger, not a gate: it names the files that moved and the
# agent does the reading. Diffing *semantics* is deliberately out of scope.
#
# WHY NOT `PreToolUse`, which the issue's Ready block specified. The two halves of
# that spelling are incompatible in this CLI build, and its own hook registry says
# so: `PreToolUse` documents exit-code channels only — 0 is "stdout/stderr not
# shown", 2 is "show stderr to model AND BLOCK tool call". The only model-facing
# channel there is the blocking one, and a deny is exactly what CLOUD-97 and
# CLOUD-219 each independently ruled out ("detection and self-clearing, never a
# hard deny"), because pausing mid-task is legitimate. `PostToolBatch` documents
# the shape verbatim: "Fires once after every tool call in a batch has resolved,
# before the next model request. Return additionalContext via hookSpecificOutput
# to inject context once for the whole batch." That is also the better moment on
# the merits — "before the next model request" IS "before the next lifecycle
# step" — and it costs one sweep per batch rather than one per tool call.
#
# MEASURED, not inferred, on the session that wrote this file: the `PostToolBatch`
# entry took effect within that same session — the edit adding it landed, and the
# next batch boundary delivered this task's `additionalContext` naming the file it
# had just changed. So the channel works, and the issue's premise ("hooks are
# session-start snapshots") is narrower than it reads: whatever the harness
# re-reads, it is not everything. That does not soften the defect — a `PreToolUse`
# guard demonstrably did NOT load mid-session, which is the measurement CLOUD-187
# was filed on — it means the reminder's settings.json sentence is a warning to
# check, not a guarantee of absence.
#
# WHY IT IS ALSO WIRED AT `SessionStart`, which is load-bearing rather than
# symmetry. The first batch of an autonomous session is routinely `git fetch` +
# rebase. A snapshot first written at the END of that batch would record
# post-rebase state and miss the very drift this exists to catch, so the seed has
# to happen before any tool runs.
#
# Fails OPEN — exit 0, no output — on everything it cannot establish, and on
# BATTEN_CONTRACT_DRIFT_BYPASS. `PostToolBatch` spends exit 2 on "stop the agentic
# loop", so a non-zero exit here would halt a turn over a bookkeeping failure.
set -uo pipefail

[ -n "${BATTEN_CONTRACT_DRIFT_BYPASS:-}" ] && exit 0

raw=$(cat) || exit 0
[ -n "$raw" ] || exit 0

# The snapshot key. Per SESSION, not per clone, because the question is "did the
# surface change since THIS session read it" — a clone-wide snapshot would remind
# a session that started *after* the change and therefore already has it.
#
# An absent `session_id` degrades to a shared key rather than to silence: a
# weaker answer beats a dead mechanism, and every event this is wired to carries
# the field today. `// empty` rather than a bare read so a payload jq cannot parse
# lands on the fallback instead of the string "null".
session=$(printf '%s' "$raw" | jq -r '.session_id // empty' 2>/dev/null) || session=""
# The event names ITSELF in the payload, so the reply's `hookEventName` is read
# from the caller rather than hardcoded — this one body is wired to two events and
# a hardcoded name would be wrong at one of them.
event=$(printf '%s' "$raw" | jq -r '.hook_event_name // empty' 2>/dev/null) || event=""
[ -n "$event" ] || event="PostToolBatch"
# Anything but the characters a session id actually uses is dropped, so the key
# can never escape the snapshot directory or name a path of its own choosing.
key=$(printf '%s' "${session:-shared}" | tr -c 'A-Za-z0-9._-' '-')
[ -n "$key" ] || key="shared"

# Outside a checkout there is no surface to hash and nowhere per-clone to keep a
# snapshot, so there is nothing this can honestly judge.
git rev-parse --show-toplevel >/dev/null 2>&1 || exit 0
git_dir=$(git rev-parse --git-dir 2>/dev/null) || exit 0
[ -n "$git_dir" ] || exit 0

# THE SURFACE, written once as data. Every path whose content binds an agent's
# conduct: the always-loaded instructions, the path-triggered rules, the hook
# wiring itself, the gate config, and the task layer those hooks resolve to.
#
# A pathspec list fed through `git ls-files`, never a shell glob: TRACKED files
# are the contract, and untracked scratch under `mise-tasks/` is not. That also
# makes an added or removed contract file drift by construction rather than
# through a second code path.
SURFACE=(AGENTS.md .claude/rules .claude/settings.json hk.pkl mise-tasks)

paths=$(git ls-files -- "${SURFACE[@]}" 2>/dev/null) || exit 0
[ -n "$paths" ] || exit 0

# One `hash-object` process for the whole surface — two spawns total regardless
# of how many files `SURFACE` resolves to, which is what makes this affordable at
# every batch boundary. The measured cost was single-digit milliseconds; the file
# count is deliberately not restated, because `SURFACE` above owns it and the
# number written here had already drifted by half before anyone read it.
#
# `--stdin-paths` rather than an argv list so the call cannot outgrow ARG_MAX as
# the task layer grows, and WORKING-TREE content rather than `git ls-tree HEAD`:
# a rebase updates the tree, and a local edit to the contract is drift too.
hashes=$(printf '%s\n' "$paths" | git hash-object --stdin-paths 2>/dev/null) || exit 0

# `<blob-sha> <path>` per line, in `ls-files` order. Zipped by line number rather
# than by a per-file call; a path containing a newline would break the pairing,
# and `git ls-files` without `-z` could not have reported one in the first place.
manifest=$(paste -d' ' <(printf '%s\n' "$hashes") <(printf '%s\n' "$paths")) || exit 0
[ -n "$manifest" ] || exit 0

# Out of tree, per clone, beside the receipt store — so a checkout stays clean and
# no new path scheme is invented.
store="$git_dir/batten-contract"
mkdir -p "$store" 2>/dev/null || exit 0
snapshot="$store/$key"

write_snapshot() {
	printf '%s\n' "$manifest" >"$snapshot" 2>/dev/null || return 1
}

# STATE 1: no snapshot. This is the session's start, so record it and say nothing.
# The seed is never a reminder — there is no "before" to have drifted from.
if [ ! -f "$snapshot" ]; then
	write_snapshot || true
	exit 0
fi

previous=$(cat "$snapshot" 2>/dev/null) || exit 0

# STATE 2: byte-identical. Silence is the default and the common case.
[ "$previous" = "$manifest" ] && exit 0

# STATE 3: it moved. One bucket for "changed or added" — a line present now and
# absent before is either a new hash for a known path or a path that did not exist,
# and both mean the same thing to a reader who has to go and look — and one for
# "no longer tracked", compared on the path field alone so a file that merely moved
# position in the listing is not reported as gone.
changed=$(
	comm -13 <(printf '%s\n' "$previous" | sort) <(printf '%s\n' "$manifest" | sort) |
		cut -d' ' -f2- | sort -u
)
gone=$(
	comm -23 \
		<(printf '%s\n' "$previous" | cut -d' ' -f2- | sort -u) \
		<(printf '%s\n' "$manifest" | cut -d' ' -f2- | sort -u)
)

# THE RATE LIMIT IS THE OVERWRITE, with no second state file to keep consistent —
# the same reasoning `stop-guard` applies to `stop_hook_active`. One reminder per
# change-set: the next invocation compares against what was just reported, so a
# surface that stops moving goes quiet by itself.
write_snapshot || true

# A hash set that differs while every path resolves to the same list is not
# possible, but a `comm` that produced nothing must not emit an empty reminder.
[ -n "$changed$gone" ] || exit 0

count=$(printf '%s\n' "$changed" | grep -c . || true)
removed_count=$(printf '%s\n' "$gone" | grep -c . || true)

# POINTER, NEVER PAYLOAD (non-negotiable rule 4), and here that is the mechanism
# as much as the rule: a reminder carrying the new text would be a mirror, and a
# mirror is satisfied by reading the hook's own output instead of the file. A
# path can only be answered by opening it. `tests/contract-drift.bats` asserts
# the emitted bytes carry no substring of the changed file's content, so a later
# edit cannot turn this into a diff.
reason="contract-drift ${count} changed, ${removed_count} removed"
if [ -n "$changed" ]; then
	reason="$reason

changed or added:
$(printf '%s\n' "$changed" | sed 's/^/  /')"
fi
if [ -n "$gone" ]; then
	reason="$reason

no longer tracked:
$(printf '%s\n' "$gone" | sed 's/^/  /')"
fi

reason="$reason

These files changed under this session, which read the OLD ones at start and has
not re-read them. Re-read the ones named above before the next lifecycle step.

If \`.claude/settings.json\` is among them, check whether a hook it adds is in this
session's wiring — a \`PreToolUse\` guard measurably was not (CLOUD-187), and an
unloaded rule is unenforced here and has to be self-enforced for the rest of this
session.

Reported once per change-set; silence otherwise. BATTEN_CONTRACT_DRIFT_BYPASS=1 to
switch it off."

jq -n --arg r "$reason" --arg e "$event" '{
  hookSpecificOutput: {
    hookEventName: $e,
    additionalContext: $r
  }
}'
